Draw the **Spectra Shard**, a 64x64 enemy-drone sprite, using only the drawing
tool.

The Shard is a **fixed-band crystalline drone** — one of the swarm of enemies in
*Spectra*, a two-band formation shooter. You are drawing the *enemy*, never the
player's ship.

## How drawing works

The only way to make a mark is the `draw` binary already on your `PATH`. It is
the sole channel: you build the sprite by calling it **one operation at a time**,
and the ordered list of operations you issue — recorded to `actions.json` — is
the **authoritative output that is scored**, not the pixels on disk. An image
produced any other way (writing a PNG directly, editing `canvas.png` by hand) is
discarded and flagged; only the recorded operations count.

After each call the binary re-renders the current image to `canvas.png` and
appends the operation to `actions.json`. A blank starting canvas and an empty log
are already in place at `{{workspace}}`.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, for example:

```
draw fill-rect --x 28 --y 28 --width 8 --height 1 --color "#ff4ec7"
```

Run `draw --help` to list every operation and `draw <operation> --help` for one
operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative contract. **Read
`canvas.png` after your calls** to see your progress and decide what to do next.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Build the Shard sprite up operation by operation until the image in `canvas.png`
matches the brief — a faceted magenta crystal carrying the diamond band glyph,
with white facet glints. When you are satisfied, stop: the recorded `actions.json`
is your finished submission.
